\subsection{Entropy Clock}

Let $\pi$ be a strictly positive stationary distribution. Use $g(p)=-D(p\Vert\pi)=-\sum_i p_i\log(p_i/\pi_i)$, with $0\log0=0$. The log-sum inequality gives $D(pK\Vert\pi K)\le D(p\Vert\pi)$, and $\pi K=\pi$, so this clock is nondecreasing. The same argument applies to the continuous semigroup. Shannon entropy $S(p)=-\sum_i p_i\log p_i$ is nondecreasing when $K$ is doubly stochastic (uniform $\pi$); irreducibility alone is insufficient. For example, the positive matrix with both rows $(3/4,1/4)$ maps $(1/2,1/2)$ to $(3/4,1/4)$ and strictly decreases Shannon entropy by strict concavity. Define $D_g f=Df/(Dg)$ only where $Dg\ne0$.

\subsection{Spectrum (Rates)}

For a finite irreducible continuous-time generator $L$, solve $L u_k = \lambda_k u_k$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\lambda_0 = 0$ (stationary mode)
    \item $\Re(\lambda_k) < 0$ for $k \geq 1$
\end{itemize}
If $L$ is diagonalizable, $f_t=\sum_k e^{\lambda_k t}c_k u_k$. Otherwise the Jordan terms also contain polynomial factors in $t$. Nonzero eigenvalues give decay rates; their negative real parts have units of inverse time.

\subsection{Invariants (Kernel Modes)}

Under the same irreducibility assumption, stationary distribution $\pi$ satisfies $\pi L = 0$. Invariant observable: $Df = 0 \iff f \in \text{Span}\{\mathbf{1}\}$. Kernel: $\ker(D) = \text{stationary subspace}$.

